# Business Website vs Social Media Page: Which Do You Need?

Your business has an Instagram page, customers contact you on WhatsApp, and you're already using social media to promote your products or services.

So why spend money building a website?

It's a question many business owners ask, especially when social media seems to be doing the job already.

You can post pictures, share updates, communicate with customers, and even receive orders without owning a website.

But here's the thing.

**A social media page and a business website aren't competing solutions. They serve different purposes, and understanding those differences can help you make better decisions for your business.**

In this article, we'll compare both options, explain their advantages and limitations, and help you decide whether your business needs a website, a social media page, or both.

## 1. What Is the Difference Between a Business Website and a Social Media Page?

Let's start with the basics.

A social media page is your business's presence on a platform such as Instagram, Facebook, TikTok, or LinkedIn.

It allows you to publish content, interact with followers, respond to messages, and build a community around your business.

A business website is a collection of pages under your own domain that you use to present your business, explain your services, showcase your work, publish information, or sell products.

For example, a fashion designer might use Instagram to share new outfits, behind-the-scenes videos, and customer pictures.

The same designer could use a website to display collections, provide sizing information, explain ordering and delivery policies, and collect enquiries or orders.

Both can help the business attract customers, but they give customers different ways to interact with the brand.

Think of social media as a place where people discover and engage with your business.

Your website can serve as the central place where they find detailed information and take the next step.

And when the two work together, your business can benefit from both.

## 2. Which Is Better for Reaching New Customers?

This depends on how your customers discover businesses like yours.

Social media can be excellent for attracting attention.

A useful video, an interesting post, a recommendation, or a share from an existing customer can introduce your business to people who have never heard of you.

You can also interact directly with potential customers, participate in relevant conversations, and build relationships over time.

However, your reach may depend on the platform's recommendation systems, your existing audience, your content, and how people engage with it.

A website offers a different opportunity.

People use search engines such as Google to look for products, services, answers, and businesses.

If your website contains relevant information and is properly optimised, it can appear in search results for queries related to your business.

For example, someone searching for a wedding photographer in Lagos might discover a photographer's website through Google.

Someone else might discover the same photographer through an Instagram Reel.

Both routes can lead to a potential customer.

The important difference is that a website gives you a place to create content specifically designed to answer the questions people are searching for.

That can include service pages, location-specific information, useful articles, portfolios, and frequently asked questions.

However, having a website doesn't guarantee that people will find it through Google. Search visibility requires relevant content, good technical foundations, and time.

**If your goal is to attract attention through social content, social media can be valuable. If you also want to build a presence in search results, a website gives you another channel to work with.**

You don't have to choose only one.

## 3. Which One Builds More Trust?

Imagine you're looking for a company to handle an important project.

You find two businesses.

The first has a social media page with several posts and a phone number.

The second has an active social media presence and a website explaining its services, showing previous projects, introducing its team, and providing clear contact information.

Which business gives you more information to help you make a decision?

For many customers, the second business makes the evaluation process easier.

A website gives you more room to explain what your business does, how you work, what customers can expect, and why they should consider your services.

You can organise your information into dedicated pages rather than relying on people to scroll through your posts.

This can be particularly useful for:

- Consultants and professional service providers.
- Photographers and creative professionals.
- Law firms and other established service businesses.
- Schools and educational institutions.
- Businesses selling high-value products.
- Companies that need to showcase projects or case studies.

That doesn't mean a website automatically makes a business trustworthy.

An outdated website, misleading claims, broken pages, or missing contact information can create the opposite impression.

Likewise, a well-maintained social media page with genuine customer feedback and helpful content can build trust.

The real goal is to give potential customers reliable information and a clear understanding of your business.

A professional website simply gives you more control over how that information is organised and presented.

## 4. Which Gives You More Control?

This is one of the most important differences.

When you create a business page on a social media platform, you're operating within that platform's environment.

The platform controls its features, rules, algorithms, and account policies.

Changes to any of these can affect how you reach your audience.

If your account becomes inaccessible or your content reaches fewer people, your ability to communicate with followers may be affected.

Your website gives you another channel that you can manage more directly.

You can choose how your pages are organised, what information appears first, how customers contact you, and what features your business needs.

You also have greater control over your domain and website content, depending on your hosting arrangement and the tools you use.

However, a website isn't completely independent of third parties. Your domain registrar, hosting provider, payment processors, and other services may still be involved.

The practical advantage is that your entire digital presence doesn't have to depend on one social media platform.

**Social media helps you build an audience on a platform. A website helps you establish a digital home for your business.**

Both can be useful, but they play different roles.

## 5. Which Is Better for Selling Products and Services?

Both can help you sell.

The better option depends on what you're selling and how you want customers to buy from you.

### Selling through social media

Social media can work well when your products are easy to showcase visually and customers are comfortable ordering through direct messages or WhatsApp.

For example, a small clothing business might post new arrivals, share customer pictures, answer questions, and receive orders through WhatsApp.

This can be a practical way to start selling without building a complete online store.

However, as your business grows, managing product information, orders, enquiries, and customer conversations across different platforms may become more difficult.

### Selling through a website

A website can give customers a more structured way to explore your products or services.

Depending on how it's built, you can include:

- Product catalogues.
- Service descriptions and packages.
- Booking forms.
- Online payment options.
- Enquiry forms.
- Customer testimonials.
- Frequently asked questions.
- Clear ordering and delivery information.

For example, a photography business could display different packages and allow potential clients to submit booking enquiries.

A bakery could display its cake options, explain ordering requirements, and direct customers to place orders.

An online store could allow customers to browse products and complete purchases without manually discussing every item with the seller.

Of course, these features require appropriate setup, and some may involve additional costs.

You don't need all of them to get started.

The goal is to choose the option that makes buying from your business easier.

## 6. What About Cost? Is Social Media Cheaper?

In terms of getting started, a social media page is often the less expensive option.

You can usually create a business profile without paying for the basic account, publish content, and start interacting with potential customers.

A website requires additional considerations.

Depending on your approach, you may need to budget for domain registration, hosting, design, development, content creation, and ongoing maintenance.

The total cost depends on the type of website you need.

A simple website introducing your business and services will generally require less investment than a custom online store with complex functionality.

But comparing the two purely by their initial cost doesn't tell the whole story.

Consider the time you spend answering repetitive questions, manually sharing product information, explaining your services, or following up with potential customers.

A well-planned website may help reduce some of that work by making important information available in one place.

It may also support enquiries, bookings, or sales outside the moments when you're actively posting on social media.

These benefits aren't automatic. They depend on the quality of your website, the way you use it, and whether it addresses real customer needs.

Before deciding, think about what your business needs today and what it might need as it grows.

