## 7. Can a Social Media Page Help Your Business Appear on Google?

Yes, social media profiles can sometimes appear in Google search results.

For example, someone searching for your business name might find your Instagram, Facebook, or LinkedIn profile.

So it's not accurate to say that social media gives your business no visibility on Google.

However, a website gives you more opportunities to create and organise content around the products, services, and questions relevant to your customers.

You can build dedicated pages for individual services, locations, products, and topics.

You can also publish helpful articles that answer questions your potential customers are already asking.

For example, a photographer could have separate pages for wedding photography, corporate photography, and portrait sessions.

A school could provide detailed information about admissions, its curriculum, facilities, and school activities.

These pages give search engines more information about what the business offers.

Your website can also provide a foundation for improving your presence in AI-powered search experiences.

If you'd like to understand that side of online visibility, read our article on [Generative Engine Optimisation (GEO): How to Optimise Your Website for AI Search](https://emmystack01.com/articles/generative-engine-optimization-geo-how-to-optimize-your-website-for-ai-search).

Remember, neither a website nor a social media profile guarantees visibility. The important thing is to provide useful, relevant information and make it accessible to the people you're trying to reach.

## 8. What If You Don't Have the Budget for a Website Yet?

You don't have to build a website immediately just because your business is online.

If you're starting with a limited budget, focus on the basics.

Create a clear business profile, explain what you offer, use good product or service images, provide accurate contact information, and make it easy for customers to enquire or place orders.

You can also encourage satisfied customers to recommend your business and share genuine feedback.

At this stage, social media may be enough to help you test your offer and understand what customers want.

As your business develops, pay attention to the limitations you begin to experience.

Are potential customers asking for information you could organise on a website?

Are you struggling to showcase all your services clearly?

Do you want to attract people who search Google for the products or services you offer?

Are you spending too much time answering the same questions?

Do you need a more structured way to receive enquiries, bookings, or orders?

If the answer to these questions is increasingly yes, it may be time to consider a website.

You don't have to build an expensive platform with every possible feature.

Start with the pages and functionality your business genuinely needs.

You can expand as your needs change.

## 9. The Best Strategy? Use Both Together

For many businesses, the most practical solution isn't choosing between a website and social media.

It's using both for what they do best.

Imagine you run a fashion brand.

You use Instagram and TikTok to showcase new collections, post styling videos, and introduce your products to new audiences.

Interested customers visit your website to explore the full collection, check sizes, read delivery information, and place orders.

You share links to relevant website pages in your social media posts and profile.

Someone who discovers your business through Google can also visit your website without needing to find one of your social media posts first.

Now imagine you're a photographer.

You use social media to share recent shoots and connect with potential clients.

Your website presents your portfolio, packages, professional background, and booking information in one place.

Or perhaps you're a consultant.

You publish useful insights on LinkedIn, while your website explains your services, showcases your experience, and provides a way for interested clients to contact you.

In each example, social media attracts attention and encourages interaction, while the website provides a more structured destination for people who want to learn more or take action.

Your exact strategy will depend on your industry, audience, and budget.

But the principle remains the same: your channels should support one another rather than operate as disconnected parts of your business.

## 10. What If You Need Something Simpler Than a Full Website?

Not every professional or entrepreneur needs a large business website.

Sometimes, what you need most is a simple, professional way to share your contact information, services, portfolio, and social links.

For example, if you regularly attend networking events, meet potential clients, or connect with people in person, you may benefit from having a digital business card.

Instead of asking people to remember your phone number, search for your social media accounts, or keep track of a paper business card, you can share one link containing your professional information.

This can make it easier for people to find and contact you after an introduction.

Emmy STACK01 offers a [Digital Business Card](https://emmystack01.com/products/digital-business-card) for professionals, creators, and entrepreneurs who want a dedicated place to present their digital identity.

It's worth understanding the distinction, though.

A digital business card is not necessarily a replacement for a complete business website.

It serves a more focused purpose: making your professional information easy to access and share.

If your main goal is to help people connect with you, a digital business card may be a useful starting point.

If you need detailed service pages, extensive content, a product catalogue, or more advanced business functionality, a full website may be more appropriate.

The right choice depends on what you're trying to achieve.

## 11. So, Which One Does Your Business Need?

Let's make the decision simpler.

### A social media page may be enough for now if:

- You're just starting your business.
- You're testing a new product or service.
- Your budget is extremely limited.
- Most of your customers already find you through social platforms.
- You can comfortably manage your enquiries and orders through your current channels.

### A website may be worth prioritising if:

- You want to attract customers through Google searches.
- You need a central place to explain your services.
- You want to showcase your portfolio or previous projects.
- You need a more structured way to generate enquiries or receive bookings.
- You want greater control over how your business information is presented.
- You want to build a longer-term online presence beyond social media.

### Using both may be the best option if:

- You already have an active social media audience.
- You want to reach people who don't follow your accounts.
- You need a professional destination for potential customers.
- You want to combine content marketing, search visibility, and social engagement.
- You're ready to build a more complete digital presence for your business.

And if you're a professional who mainly needs to share contact information and make networking easier, a digital business card may be a useful addition to either approach.

The decision shouldn't be based on what every other business is doing.

It should be based on your goals, your customers, and the problems you need to solve.

## Final Thoughts: Don't Choose a Platform. Choose What Your Business Needs.

A social media page can help your business attract attention, communicate with customers, and build an audience.

A website can give your business a central place to present information, build visibility through search, showcase your work, and support customer enquiries or sales.

Neither one guarantees business growth on its own.

What matters is how well you use each channel to serve your customers and achieve your goals.

If you're just starting out, social media may be enough to get moving.

As your business grows, a website can help you build on that foundation.

And when you combine the two strategically, you create more ways for people to discover your business, understand what you offer, and decide whether they want to work with you.

**You don't need to be everywhere. You need to be where your customers can find you, and you need to give them a clear next step when they do.**

